<div class="topic" data-i="0"><button class="th" type="button" aria-expanded="false"><span class="ic"><svg><use href="#i11"/></svg></span><span><b>What you will learn</b><small>Four outcomes for this module</small></span><span class="ch"></span></button><div class="pn"><p class="cs-lead">By the end of this module you will be able to:</p><ol class="nums"><li>Describe COSECSA, its member countries and its training programmes.</li><li>State when the Membership and Fellowship examinations take place and what each contains.</li><li>Explain why the examinations changed format as candidate numbers grew.</li><li>Tell the examination formats apart: Entrust, online OSCE, patient-based OSCE, patientless OSCE and viva voce.</li></ol></div></div>

<div class="topic" data-i="1"><button class="th" type="button" aria-expanded="false"><span class="ic"><svg><use href="#i10"/></svg></span><span><b>What is COSECSA?</b><small>Mission, countries and the 14 specialties</small></span><span class="ch"></span></button><div class="pn"><p class="cs-lead">The College of Surgeons of East, Central and Southern Africa is a non-profit professional body whose mission is to promote excellence in surgical training, care and research.</p>
<div class="stats"><div><b>1999</b><span>year COSECSA was established</span></div><div><b>15</b><span>member countries</span></div><div><b>9</b><span>partner countries</span></div><div><b>162</b><span>accredited hospitals</span></div></div>
<div class="grid2"><div class="cs-card"><h3>Member countries</h3><p>Botswana, Burundi, Ethiopia, Kenya, Lesotho, Malawi, Mozambique, Namibia, Rwanda, South Sudan, Sudan, Tanzania, Uganda, Zambia, Zimbabwe.</p></div>
<div class="cs-card"><h3>Partner countries</h3><p>Angola, Cameroon, D.R. Congo, Eswatini, Gabon, Madagascar, Niger, Somaliland, Togo.</p></div></div>
<p>The Secretariat is headquartered in Arusha, Tanzania. COSECSA provides postgraduate surgical training through Programme Directors and Trainers in accredited hospitals across sub-Saharan Africa.</p>

<h3>What COSECSA trains</h3>
<p>COSECSA trains at Membership level (Basic Surgery, MCS) and in 14 Fellowship specialties.</p>
<figure style="margin:0"><img id="specimg" alt="COSECSA now offers 14 specialities: General Surgery, Orthopaedics, Urology, Paediatric Surgery, Otorhinolaryngology, Plastic Surgery, Neurosurgery, Paediatric Orthopaedics, Cardiothoracic Surgery, Breast Surgery, Upper GI Surgery, Lower GI Surgery, Surgical Endoscopy and Thoracic Surgery" style="width:100%;height:auto;border-radius:10px;display:block"></figure></div></div>

<div class="topic" data-i="2"><button class="th" type="button" aria-expanded="false"><span class="ic"><svg><use href="#i9"/></svg></span><span><b>The two examinations</b><small>Membership and Fellowship at a glance</small></span><span class="ch"></span></button><div class="pn"><div class="scroll cs-card"><table>
<tr><th></th><th>Membership (MCS)</th><th>Fellowship (FCS)</th></tr>
<tr><td>When in training</td><td>End of the second year of surgical training</td><td>After specialty training; candidates have passed FCS Part I</td></tr>
<tr><td>When held</td><td>Mid-November, fully online</td><td>End of November to early December, over 2 days</td></tr>
<tr><td>Components</td><td>Entrust examination and online OSCE</td><td>Patient-based OSCE, patientless OSCE, viva voce</td></tr>
<tr><td>Examiners</td><td>One or two per station (Module 5)</td><td>Two per station and per viva room (Module 6)</td></tr>
</table></div></div></div>

<div class="topic" data-i="3"><button class="th" type="button" aria-expanded="false"><span class="ic"><svg><use href="#i19"/></svg></span><span><b>The Membership examination</b><small>Entrust and online OSCE, with the Entrust story</small></span><span class="ch"></span></button><div class="pn"><p>The Membership examination is taken at the end of the second year of surgical training. It used to be an in-person OSCE with real patients or actors. Because the number of candidates grew, it is now delivered online. It has two parts.</p>
<div class="grid2"><div class="cs-card"><span class="tag ent">Part 1</span><h3>Entrust examination</h3><p>An online, case-based assessment conducted through the Entrust platform. Candidates sit eight Entrust stations. No examiners take part.</p></div>
<div class="cs-card"><span class="tag zm">Part 2</span><h3>Online OSCE</h3><p>Eight Zoom stations with examiners, held on the first day. It assesses clinical knowledge, clinical reasoning, decision-making and applying knowledge to clinical scenarios. See Module 5.</p></div></div>
<h3>A short history of Entrust</h3>
<p>Entrust is a virtual patient simulation platform developed through a collaboration at Stanford University. Each case has two components: a virtual patient simulation, then multiple-choice questions.</p>
<div class="grid2">
<div class="cs-card"><span class="tag ent">2021</span><h3>Introduced into the MCS examination</h3><p>Entrust became part of the COSECSA Membership (MCS) examination.</p></div>
<div class="cs-card"><span class="tag ent">2025</span><h3>Practice cases for trainees</h3><p>COSECSA developed MCS practice case scenarios so trainees can learn on the platform before the examination.</p></div>
</div>
<div class="scroll cs-card"><table><tr><th>Component</th><th>What the candidate does</th><th>Time (trainee orientation)</th></tr>
<tr><td>1. Virtual patient simulation</td><td>Reviews the history and examination, orders labs and imaging, asks for consults, chooses interventions and a disposition</td><td>10 minutes</td></tr>
<tr><td>2. Multiple-choice questions</td><td>Answers five questions about the case</td><td>5 minutes</td></tr></table></div>
<div class="grid2">
